{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler who reigned as Count of Tripoli from 1155 until his death in 1193. He was a significant figure in the Crusader states and a key player in the political landscape of the Levant during the 12th and 13th centuries. His reign marked a period of both prosperity and conflict for the County of Tripoli.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was the son of Raymond I Count of Tripoli and his wife Alix of Montfort. He inherited the County upon his father's death in 1155.  His early reign saw him consolidating power and strengthening the County's defenses. He skillfully navigated the complex political alliances of the time particularly with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Byzantine Empire. Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his willingness to engage in warfare to protect Tripoli's interests. He fostered trade and agriculture leading to economic growth in the region.\n\nRaymond III’s reign was characterized by numerous military campaigns. He engaged in conflicts with the Seljuk Turks and other Muslim powers seeking to expand Tripoli's territory and influence. He also fought against various internal rebellions and rival factions within the County. His military successes helped to maintain Tripoli’s independence and secure its position as a major power in the Levant.  He also participated in several Crusades although his involvement was sometimes controversial.  He was a staunch defender of the Christian faith and actively supported the Crusader cause.\n\n*Diplomacy and Foreign Policy*  Raymond III was a skilled diplomat who maintained relations with numerous European powers. He sought alliances with various kingdoms and principalities to secure Tripoli's borders and ensure its economic prosperity.  He formed strategic partnerships with the Holy Roman Empire and the Papal States. He also engaged in extensive trade with Venice and Genoa benefiting from their maritime power and commercial networks.  Raymond’s diplomatic efforts were crucial in preserving Tripoli's independence and maintaining its position as a significant trading center. He skillfully used diplomacy to manage internal conflicts and prevent civil wars.\n\n*Legacy and Death*  Raymond III's reign was considered a golden age for the County of Tripoli.  His military victories economic prosperity and diplomatic achievements established Tripoli as a prominent power in the region. He left behind a stable and prosperous county that continued to thrive for several decades after his death.  He died in 1193 at the age of 68.  His legacy as a strong and capable ruler continued to influence the political landscape of the Levant for generations to come.  He is remembered as one of the most successful and influential Counts of Tripoli.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1804991215467453}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -6.115249561844394e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.08474750816822052}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.03216259181499481}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5741356611251831}, {"token": "▁ruler", "logprob": -0.6246256232261658}, {"token": "▁who", "logprob": -0.6148910522460938}, {"token": "▁reigned", "logprob": -0.5363537669181824}, {"token": "▁as", "logprob": -0.8773558735847473}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -0.0009899006690829992}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -0.0009485750924795866}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": -4.827859811484814e-05}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0011508992174640298}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.00358522217720747}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00011526874004630372}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.0009358317474834621}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.8982092142105103}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.030